# Renode simulation of the ScanWatch 2 (HWA10)

At the moment there are patches needed, so it's only been verified on firmware version 3411

It simulates the main SoC (nRF52840) running the three images in flash

- Nordic's MBR (forwards interrupts and SVCs)
- S140 SoftDevice (Nordic's BLE stack and SoC services)
- The application

It models some of the peripherals:

- external SPI flash
- battery ADC
- SSD1320 OLED

It boots the whole application (RTOS, BLE advertising, algorithm manager, display).

At the moment, it's not very fast due to some hacks; in the future, ideally, it will be
fast enough to run live as a simulator. Maybe it's too ambitious and it's only good
as integration testing. We'll see.

## Dependencies

  - Renode 1.17
  - llvm-objdump/llvm-objcopy and python3
  - Firmware file for v3411
  - A dump of the SPI flash
  - [NRF52840.svd](https://dl.antmicro.com/projects/renode/svd/NRF52840.svd.gz)

## How to run

Once, extract the 'partitions' from the package with `extract.py`

To run the simulator with display, run

```bash
renode --disable-xwt --console -e "include @display-run.resc" < <(sleep 1000)
```

It will create these files at `out/`:

 - uart0.log
 - frames/frameNNN.bin
 - oled_stream.txt

If you want to render the .bin files to png:

```bash
python3 render_png.py --w 144 --h 98 --fmt gray4 out/frames/frame400.bin out/frame400.png
```

## Debug stuff

To read the disassembly, build it with `mkdis.sh`; it writes `out/appl.dis` and
`out/sd.dis` with absolute flash addresses, so they line up with `symbols.txt`
(the address to name map for the app, MBR and SoftDevice).

To find where the boot stalls, generate hooks for a code range with
`calltrace.py 0x2e700 0x2e900 > out/hooks.resc`, include that file in a copy of
`display-run.resc`, and look at the last function entered.

The peripheral models are the `Nrf*.cs` and `OledSpim.cs` files, one per
peripheral; the run script sets their inputs (battery volts, temperature).
The earlier bring-up scripts (`boot.resc`, `run-logs.resc`, `trace.resc`,
`logs.resc`, `coverage.py`) still work but `display-run.resc` replaced them.
